WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:00.800
Texas now,

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.801
where parts of the Dallas

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.903
Fort Worth area look like ghost towns

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:04.804
as an ice storm

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:05.672
wreaks havoc

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.741
on much of the southern and central U.S.

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:08.475
Right now,

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:10.443
most people are heeding the warnings

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.978
and avoiding the roads

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.346
after treacherous conditions

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.682
sent drivers sliding out of control.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:16.549
Crews say

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:17.584
they have responded to more

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.220
than 100 traffic related emergency calls.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.855
At least two people across

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:23.656
Texas have been killed.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:25.291
The freezing weather also paralyzing

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:25.925
air travel.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.128
More than 1700 flights canceled

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:30.397
and more than 2000 are delayed.

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:31.031
Right now,

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.033
40 million people are under winter

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:33.833
weather alerts

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:34.401
as officials

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:35.568
warn ice accumulation

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.771
could also lead to power outages.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:38.738
But Texas Governor Greg

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.873
Abbott assured residents

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:40.640
earlier today

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.876
that the state's power grid is working

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.178
and no problems are expected.

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.646
Let's bring in CNN meteorologist

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:48.048
Jennifer Gray and CNN's Ed

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.783
Lavandera live in Dallas.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.384
Will start in Dallas.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:52.852
What are you hearing from people there at

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:55.688
Well, you know,

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:56.222
as you mentioned,

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.123
I think a lot of people

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.758
are simply heeding those warnings

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.393
and not coming out

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.595
in the droves out onto the streets here.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.464
And that is because the roadways

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:06.533
really are quite a mess here.

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:07.834
We are standing over Highway

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.136
75 on the north side of Dallas.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.838
And, you know, we have seen,

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.473
you know, trucks and cars

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:14.007
taking it

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:15.208
extremely slowly

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:17.077
out here on these highways.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.446
And you have really seen the sleet

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:20.780
that fell several hours ago.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.082
We have only had one

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.584
initial wave of intense sleet and ice,

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:26.820
icy rain here in the area

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.054
throughout the day.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:29.589
But it is really

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.291
hardened up on these highways.

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:33.726
And as we go into tomorrow,

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.029
that is clearly going to be a sign of

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:37.664
of great concern

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.466
because this is going to be even

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.234
even more slippery

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:41.868
come tomorrow

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:44.337
after all of this hardens and intense

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:45.772
overnight freeze.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:46.906
But it has been treacherous

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:47.640
and dangerous

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.943
for people who have been traveling.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.044
We spoke with

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.680
Amber and Jason

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.247
Kelley, who

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.316
were driving from Wichita Falls

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:57.684
to Fort Worth.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:58.651
They left last night

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.086
hoping to beat the storm.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:01.121
They got stranded

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.189
almost a little bit past halfway

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.158
They spent their entire night

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.426
in the freezing temperatures

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:07.527
in their car,

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.028
trying to conserve as much gas

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:09.629
as possible

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.265
to keep that car heater going.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.902
Decided to leave last night about nine 30

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:20.306
and which would be a normal hour.

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.109
40 52 hour ride has already been,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.411
what, 8 hours, I guess.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:28.748
And I'm sure we have at least three

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:29.916
to four more hours.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.050
I think we came to a standstill

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.253
about 11 p.m.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:35.755
and we didn't move not one inch for

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:41.227
7 hours and something 11, 12 hours.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.796
We didn't move not one inch

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:45.798
That drive

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.367
two ended up taking them 12 hours.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.501
They made it safely

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.836
but they were stranded there

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:50.136
on that

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:52.472
highway with hundreds of other cars

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:53.973
that just weren't moving

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:55.141
And that is the situation

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:56.876
that people are dealing with here today.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:57.977
Victor Nelson.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.879
Oh my gosh, what an ordeal.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:00.180
All right.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:00.847
So Jennifer,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.081
what can we expect

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.516
in the next couple of days?

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:03.950
Well, guys,

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:04.651
unfortunately, we're

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:05.552
just going to continue

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:07.220
to get waves of this once

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.821
this ends in Dallas.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:10.790
We're going to see another wave of this

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:11.758
by tomorrow.

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:12.458
And freezing

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.761
rain is the worst case scenario

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.496
when it comes to winter precipitation

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:18.831
because it latches on and freezes

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.133
to all the surfaces,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.467
whether it's the roads,

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:22.135
your car,

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.671
the power lines, the trees, anything.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:25.705
And so it's creating

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.707
an incredibly dangerous situation.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.609
As temperatures stay below freezing,

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:30.777
we got another round of this.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:32.612
So it's just going to be compounded

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:34.347
over the next day or two.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:36.015
Little Rock in this as well.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:36.616
And you can see

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:38.351
it's already moving into Memphis.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.385
So that's another area

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.553
we're going to be watching.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:41.187
And you can see

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:42.121
just looking at the roads,

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.423
it doesn't look like much.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:45.291
So it may catch you by surprise,

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.993
but you are going to slide.

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:50.129
There's no way you can drive over ice.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.131
It is just incredibly slippery,

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.399
especially for those bridges

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:54.234
and overpasses.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.436
We could see up to a half an inch of ice

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.571
in those areas shaded in blue,

00:03:58.571 --> 00:03:59.839
about a quarter of an inch.

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:00.607
And the pink,

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:04.043
you see all the ice reports of sleet

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:04.877
and ice

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:07.046
anywhere from Texas to the Ohio Valley.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.615
So this is far reaching.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:09.849
We have seen more than a quarter

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:10.850
of an inch of ice across

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.052
Arkansas, Missouri, Texas,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.687
about a quarter of an inch to a half

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.689
an inch will break those tree limbs.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:17.156
Guys,

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:18.491
we could see power outages

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.325
from this as well,

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.027
especially as we get into tomorrow

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:22.695
and we go through our wet third

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.797
round of this in just a couple of days.

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:26.466
All right.

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:28.835
Jennifer Gray, Ed Lavendera, thank you.

